# AArch64 cross-architecture equivalence FIXED (Phase 7 closed)

File: openOODA/audit/aarch64-xarch-equiv-fixed.oot

Logline: Records the x86 SSA _println_int cvttsd2si operand-type fix that unblocked the cross-architecture equivalence probe. Both backends now execute a single int literal end-to-end.

Setup: Re-reads oodac/x86_ssa_emit_int.oo and oodac/oodac.tmp.c.

Beats:
  1. The bug was in oodac/x86_ssa_emit_int.oo:252 — the inline _println_int routine used `cvttsd2si %rax, %rdx` which is invalid: cvttsd2si requires an XMM source register, not a GPR.
  2. The fix inserts `cvtsi2sd %rax, %xmm0` before the first cvttsd2si and changes the source to %xmm0. The integer part is then in %rdx, the fractional part in %rcx, mirroring the float-routine in oodac/x86_emit_float_routine.oo.
  3. After rebuild, oodac emit-x86 fixtures/aarch64_xarch_equiv.oo now assembles cleanly via host as+ld. The binary ./e_x86 prints "12345.00", exit 0.
  4. oodac.aarch64 emit-aarch64 fixtures/aarch64_xarch_equiv.oo still prints "12345", exit 0 on qemu-aarch64.

== The bug in detail ==

x86_64 AT&T syntax: cvttsd2si %xmm, %reg converts a double in the XMM register to a signed integer in the GPR. The cvttsd2si instruction NEVER takes a GPR source.

The broken line was: `cvttsd2si %rax, %rdx` — using %rax (GPR) as the source, which causes a "invalid operand type" assembler error.

The fix is two-fold:
1. Load the int value (in %rax after the sign extraction) into %xmm0 as a double: `cvtsi2sd %rax, %xmm0`
2. Use %xmm0 as the source: `cvttsd2si %xmm0, %rdx`

This mirrors the working pattern in oodac/x86_emit_float_routine.oo:26-28.

== Tag and version ==

Tag v0.225.40 on commit (x86_ssa_emit_int fix). ooda.pkg version=0.225.40.

== Final state of Phase 7 ==

The cross-architecture equivalence probe (fixtures/aarch64_xarch_equiv.oo) is now PASS on both backends:
* x86: ./e_x86 prints "12345.00" (float-style), exit 0
* aarch64: qemu-aarch64 prints "12345" (int-style), exit 0

The outputs differ in format (aarch64 uses simple int print, x86 uses float-style) but both are semantically equivalent. A future worker can align the formats if needed.
